"Prudential launches brand new AI Underwriter powered by Alibaba Cloud | Prudential HK (https://www.prudential.com.hk/en/about-us/newsroom/prudential-launches-brand-new-ai-underwriter-powered-by-alibaba-cloud/)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 6 days ago;  (Hong Kong, 9 September 2026) Prudential Hong Kong Limited (“Prudential”) today announced the full launch of AI Underwriter, an AI-powered underwriting solution developed in collaboration with Alibaba Cloud, a global leading provider of AI infrastructure and the intelligence backbone of Alibaba Group, making preliminary underwriting guidance available to all Prudential financial consultants within minutes.\n\n  * # Prudential launches brand new AI Underwriter powered by Alibaba Cloud\n\nNew service and partnership to enhance customer experience and reinforce Prudential’s leadership in AI-powered insurance innovation\n\n09-09-2026\n\nKey highlights\n\n  * Prudential launches brand new AI Underwriter together with Alibaba Cloud\n  * Enables financial consultants to obtain preliminary underwriting guidance, reducing assessment time from several days to just minutes\n  * Developed and deployed in less than three months, with plans to expand to additional distribution channels\n\n \n\n (Hong Kong, 9 September 2026) Prudential Hong Kong Limited (“Prudential”) today announced the full launch of AI Underwriter, an AI-powered underwriting solution developed in collaboration with Alibaba Cloud, a global leading provider of AI infrastructure and the intelligence backbone of Alibaba Group, making preliminary underwriting guidance available to all Prudential financial consultants within minutes.\n\nThe launch of AI Underwriter represents a significant milestone in Prudential’s application of artificial intelligence (AI) to transform the insurance journey. Combining Prudential’s underwriting expertise with Alibaba Cloud’s AI capabilities, AI Underwriter enables financial consultants to obtain preliminary underwriting indications through a chatbot at the point of sale, based on information such as a customer’s financial, medical, occupational and residential profile.\n\nThe AI solution can provide preliminary indications on acceptance, exclusion, loading or additional information required within minutes, helping financial consultants better understand potential underwriting requirements before application submission and giving customers greater clarity and peace of mind earlier in the journey.\n\nLawrence Lam, Chief Executive Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited, said: “AI Underwriter marks an important step forward in how Prudential works with leading ecosystem partners to bring practical innovation into the insurance journey. Our collaboration with Alibaba Cloud reflects our belief that the future of insurance will be shaped not by technology alone, but by how effectively we combine industry expertise, trusted partnerships and real-world execution. For us, this is what AI leadership means — making insurance simpler, faster and stronger for customers and our distribution channels. Above all, AI enables us to anticipate customer needs and act earlier, delivering greater transparency and better outcomes.”\n\nEthan Yuan, Vice President of International Business, Regional General Manager of APAC North, Alibaba Cloud Intelligence, said: “AI Underwriter exemplifies how full-stack AI — from cloud infrastructure to foundation models  — can deliver tangible values in one of the most knowledge-intensive functions in insurance sector. This collaboration merges Prudential’s domain expertise with Alibaba Cloud’s trusted capabilities, proving that AI can successfully transition from pilot programs to core business operations. It also sets a compelling benchmark for AI-powered innovation across financial services in the region.”\n\nPreliminary underwriting assessment time reduced to just minutes\n\nBefore AI Underwriter, it could take days for a preliminary review to indicate what additional information may be required before an application proceeds. Built in close collaboration with Alibaba Cloud, the solution brings together Prudential’s business, underwriting and technology expertise to translate underwriting rules, experience and business needs into a practical AI-enabled tool for real-world use by financial consultants. By helping them receive initial guidance earlier, collect more complete information from customers upfront and reduce administrative back-and-forth, AI Underwriter supports a smoother submission process and ultimately makes protection faster for customers.\n\nCandy Au Yeung, Chief Customer Operations and Health Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited, said: “The future of customer experience lies in combining human expertise with intelligent AI. AI Underwriter gives our distribution partners clarity on pre-underwriting outcomes earlier, helps customers secure protection sooner, and enables more consistent decisions with the professional judgement customers expect from Prudential. It also reflects how we are reimagining our operating model: through a forward-deployment engineering approach, our AI, Technology and Project teams work closely with the business to build practical, scalable AI solutions. Alibaba Cloud empowers enterprises worldwide with its full-stack AI capabilities, powered by Qwen (Chinese: Qianwen), a family of large language and multimodal AI models developed by Alibaba. Debuted in 2023, Qwen models are available to global developers via HuggingFace (open-weight) or through API on Model Studio, Alibaba’s one-stop, enterprise-grade AI development platform offering access to the full suite of Qwen foundation models. \n\nPrudential and Alibaba Cloud partner to launch the new AI-powered underwriting tool “AI Underwriter”. Guests in attendance at the press conference include (from left to right):\n\n  * Candy Au Yeung, Chief Customer Operations and Health Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited\n  * Lawrence Lam, Chief Executive Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited\n  * Ethan Yuan, Vice President of International Business, Regional General Manager of APAC North, Alibaba Cloud Intelligence\n\nImage: 相片1\n\nPrudential and Alibaba Cloud partner to launch the new AI-powered underwriting tool “AI Underwriter”. Guests in attendance at the press conference include (from left to right):\n\n  * Candy Au Yeung, Chief Customer Operations and Health Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited\n  * Lawrence Lam, Chief Executive Officer, Prudential Hong Kong Limited\n  * Ethan Yuan, Vice President of International Business, Regional General Manager of APAC North, Alibaba Cloud Intelligence\n\nImage: 相片2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow Intact is measuring AI's annual benefits (https://canadianunderwriter.ca/your-business/operations/how-intact-is-measuring-ais-annual-benefits/)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; By Jason Contant, | September 11, 2026 | Last updated on September 11, 2026 ... Intact Financial Corporation (IFC) is seeing clear financial gain from its use of artificial intelligence (AI), “in aggregate currently producing north of $220 million of recurring benefit annually that we can measure,” IFC chief operating officer Patrick Barbeau said Thursday.About a year-and-a-half ago, Intact anticipated the number would grow to $500 million by 2030, but has since revised that estimate to $500 million in annualized financial benefits by 2028, Barbeau said during a fireside chat at the 27^{th} annual Scotiabank Financials Summit in Toronto.\n\nHow Intact is measuring AI’s annual benefits\n\n# How Intact is measuring AI’s annual benefits\n\nIntact has revised its annualized estimated financial benefits from AI because it’s seeing better outcomes than expected\n\nBy Jason Contant, | September 11, 2026 | Last updated on September 11, 2026\n\nImage: Financial benefits of AI.\n\niStock.com/Phiwath Jittamas\n\nIntact Financial Corporation (IFC) is seeing clear financial gain from its use of artificial intelligence (AI), “in aggregate currently producing north of $220 million of recurring benefit annually that we can measure,” IFC chief operating officer Patrick Barbeau said Thursday.\n\nAbout a year-and-a-half ago, Intact anticipated the number would grow to $500 million by 2030, but has since revised that estimate to $500 million in annualized financial benefits by 2028, Barbeau said during a fireside chat at the 27^{th} annual Scotiabank Financials Summit in Toronto.\n\n“We’ve accelerated our investment recently, and we’re saying that the benefit on the pricing and risk selection side in commercial and specialty lines is actually producing [a] better outcome than we even anticipated at the time,” Barbeau says.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXPT Specialty Deploys Suite of AI Tools Across Wholesale Operation; Widens Market Access for Retail Agents on SME Accounts | XPT Specialty (https://xptspecialty.com/xpt-specialty-deploys-suite-of-ai-tools-across-wholesale-operation/)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; October 1, 2026 – XPT Specialty, a leading wholesale broker for specialty commercial and personal insurance, has deployed a suite of AI tools that help retail agency partners better serve SME accounts – broadening market access and returning more bound policies on a segment historically hardest to serve at scale. ... A recent JD Power study found a 203-point satisfaction gap between SMBs whose insurer and broker truly understand their business and those who do not.\n\n# XPT Specialty Deploys Suite of AI Tools Across Wholesale Operation; Widens Market Access for Retail Agents on SME Accounts\n\n  * October 1, 2026\n\nAI tools bring comparative analysis and multi-market execution into retail agents’ existing workflows, helping XPT specialists serve more clients, faster and with greater precision and insight\n\n## Executive Summary\n\n  * What: Three AI tools –\n    * Underwriting: comparative data analysis and quoting;\n    * Binding: comparative rating with multi-market execution, and\n    * Brokerage: risk analysis, market finder and multi-market execution\n  * Why it matters: The tools absorb small and mid-sized enterprise (SME) processing on the wholesale side, putting up to 20 markets in front of accounts that used to receive the narrowest search.\n  * Early results: in P&C, bound business jumped as much as 19.5% month-over-month, and, year-over-year, bind ratios are increasing; submissions overall are steady, while quotes are up by 6.5% year-over-year and the number of binds is up about 7.5% year-over-year since inception; in Bars and Taverns, new-business quoting is up approximately 25% year-over-year.\n  * For retail agents: No new portal, no re-keying. XPT specialists review every risk and remain accountable for final placement.\n\nOctober 1, 2026 – XPT Specialty, a leading wholesale broker for specialty commercial and personal insurance, has deployed a suite of AI tools that help retail agency partners better serve SME accounts – broadening market access and returning more bound policies on a segment historically hardest to serve at scale. XPT’s specialists guide every risk and placement decision.\n\n“Small commercial accounts can take nearly as much work as large ones but carry a fraction of the premium, so the market has quietly underserved them for years,” said Kyle Stevens, executive partner at XPT. “These tools let us put more markets in front of more accounts and give our people more room to apply their expertise.”\n\n“Working with XPT gives us access to a broader range of markets and more options for our clients,” said Derek S. Ross, president and COO of Kulchin Ross Insurance Services, LLC. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntact Financial at Scotiabank summit: ai, capital and growth By Investing.com (https://www.investing.com/news/transcripts/intact-financial-at-scotiabank-summit-ai-capital-and-growth-93CH-4896618)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; On Thursday, 10 September 2026, Intact Financial Corporation (IFC) used Scotiabank’s 27th Annual Financials Summit to outline a business that is growing, investing and still facing pockets of pressure. ...   * The company said artificial intelligence is already generating more than CAD 220 million in recurring annual benefits, and it now expects CAD 500 million in recurring pre-tax annualized revenue from AI by 2028, two years earlier than planned.\n\n# Intact Financial at Scotiabank summit: ai, capital and growth\n\nPublished Sep 10, 2026, 01:30 PM\n\nIntact Financial at Scotiabank summit: ai, capital and growth\n\nIntact Financial at Scotiabank summit: ai, capital and growth\n\nImage: &copy; Reuters.\n\n© Reuters.\n\nIn this article:\n\nIFC-1.64%\n\nOn Thursday, 10 September 2026, Intact Financial Corporation (IFC) used Scotiabank’s 27th Annual Financials Summit to outline a business that is growing, investing and still facing pockets of pressure. Chief Operating Officer Patrick Barbeau said the insurer is benefiting from disciplined pricing and scale, even as it works through integration tasks in the UK and Ireland and watches inflation and catastrophe losses in several markets.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n  * Intact said its 2025 return on equity performance widened to 740 basis points, above a 500 basis point target, while decade-long net operating income per share growth reached a 12% compound annual rate.\n  * The company said artificial intelligence is already generating more than CAD 220 million in recurring annual benefits, and it now expects CAD 500 million in recurring pre-tax annualized revenue from AI by 2028, two years earlier than planned.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow we helped an insurance underwriting group add $1.96M in net recurring profit. | Already Built AI (https://alreadybuilt.ai/case-studies/insurance-underwriting-operations)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: today; Commercial Insurance · Submission intake and carrier reporting ... By Already Built AI team Updated October 3, 2026 ... And so we started with a one-week study in which underwriters, assistants, operations staff, and reporting staff logged their work.\n\nCommercial Insurance · Submission intake and carrier reporting\n\n# How we helped an insurance underwriting group add $1.96M in net recurring profit.\n\nGrowth was adding more preparation and reporting work around every underwriting decision. We connected both tasks through one submission record, cutting monthly reporting from 40 to 10 staff-hours per program. Their finance team confirmed the profit increase.\n\nBy Already Built AI team Updated October 3, 2026\n\n## The results\n\nAdded net recurring profit\n    $1.96M\n    We helped their group turn growth into more recurring profit, which their finance team measured independently.\n\nAdditional commission\n    $2.4M\n    Their group recorded $16M more bound premium from incoming submissions. The related commission was separate from the profit result.\n\nMonthly report hours per program\n    40 → 10\n    We removed 30 staff-hours of recurring work from each program, so teams spent less time assembling information they already held.\n\nTheir group wanted more underwriting business, but their underwriters were spending ~33% of their week on intake, clearance, and chasing missing information. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCase study: Ten months of EvryAI inside a health plan · Pendral (https://pendral.ai/case-studies/evry-health-ai)\nciteturn1search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Case study · Evry Health · Figures to 31 July 2026# Ten months of enterprise AI inside a health plan, measured. ... The platform went in front of the whole company in October. ... Finance  | Form 5500 and Schedule A prepared for a group insurance client from live enrollment data  | 58-message session, 14 distinct tools ... Every figure comes from a full census of production records between 1 October 2025 and 31 July 2026, not a sample or a survey.\n\nCase study · Evry Health · Figures to 31 July 2026\n\n# Ten months of enterprise AI inside a health plan, measured.\n\nWhat ten months of real usage across 108 employees produced inside Evry Health, measured from a full census of production records rather than surveyed, and what it suggests for any regulated company weighing the same move.\n\nScope: the EvryAI chat platform only. Vocalis, Inline, Lakefront and Bellweather are separate systems and none of their usage is counted here. Download the full report (PDF).\n\nmessages\n\n87,684\n\nfrom 108 employees across 10,472 conversations\n\nmonthly adoption\n\n87%\n\nof the whole company, and still rising\n\nvalue created\n\n~$1M/yr\n\nhours displaced, new capability, spend avoided, compute below list\n\ntokens processed\n\n12.5B\n\nof real work, 5.3B in July alone\n\n## What ten months produced\n\n  1. 1 Nearly nine in ten of the company uses it every month, and the number is still rising. 94 of 108 people in July, up from 2 last October, with no month yet showing a decline.\n  2. 2 It has produced 13,497 finished documents. More than one per conversation. Four in ten are Word files, spreadsheets and decks that went on to be used as real work.\n  3. 3 It is doing work that could not otherwise have been done. A URAC accreditation submission, an ORSA model, a reserve method validated against six months of actual claim runout, a 21-state employee handbook.\n  4. 4 Total value created is approaching $1M a year: $254,000 in time recovered ($2,702 per active user), a comparable band of work that would not have happened otherwise, avoided spend, and roughly $300K a year of list-rate model compute delivered for under $10K a month.\n\n## Adoption climbed for ten straight months\n\nThe platform went in front of the whole company in October. Ten months later 87% of employees use it every month and adoption is still climbing. It has produced 13,497 finished work products across 10,472 conversations, which is more than one deliverable per conversation. The most valuable behaviour found in the record is that it pushes back: it withdrew its own reserving recommendation after testing showed it was $1.65M short, read the plan’s own policy wording more strictly than the team had, and declined a shortcut on member notices that the Texas delivery rule would not allow.\n\nMessages per month\n\nTen months of growth with no sign of levelling off. July was the largest month, at 21,237 messages.\n\nPeople using it each month\n\nFrom 2 people to 94, out of 108 employees. Shown separately from volume so the two are not confused.\n\n  * The routing layer absorbs model change. Five generation changes in nine months without a migration project. Nothing had to be re-platformed to stay current.\n\nCapability is easy to demonstrate and hard to trust. What is rarer is a production transcript of a system withdrawing a $1.65M recommendation after its own back-test, declining to pass a standard the team wanted passed, or catching that a proposed shortcut would not meet a state insurance rule. In front of a risk committee that carries further than an accuracy benchmark, because it can be read end to end.\n\n## How the numbers were produced\n\nEvery figure comes from a full census of production records between 1 October 2025 and 31 July 2026, not a sample or a survey. Ten analysts each examined one department, nine flagship conversations were read in full, and four further passes reconciled the results. Both sides of every conversation were read: 10,472 conversations, 87,684 messages and all 43,763 responses the platform sent, around 27 million tokens of output. Behavioural rates such as self-correction were detected automatically, then a sample of each graded by hand and the rate adjusted to the measured precision. Time savings are modelled from stated assumptions about how long each task takes by hand; the reserving work is measured against six months of actual claim runout. Every script is committed and the whole analysis can be re-run. Supporting analysis is available under NDA.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLemonade Case Study: Rebuilding Insurance Around AI | G&CO. (https://www.g-co.agency/insights/lemonade-case-study-rebuilding-insurance-around-ai)\nciteturn1search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 days ago; Crawled: today; October 1, 2026 ... This case study looks at how Lemonade rebuilt insurance around artificial intelligence rather than bolting technology onto an old process, and paired that with a business model designed to align its interests with its customers'. ... The strategic choice at the heart of this case is that Lemonade did not set out to be a nicer insurance company; it set out to rebuild insurance around two ideas the incumbents could not easily adopt, AI doing the work and a flat fee removing the conflict, because their old systems and profit models were built the other way. ... Read at scale, Lemonade is a case about rebuilding a legacy industry around AI and incentives rather than digitizing it, and it connects to the broader shifts in insurtech, AI-driven operations, customer experience, and trust.\n\n# Lemonade Case Study: Rebuilding Insurance Around AI\n\nLast Updated:\n\nOctober 1, 2026\n\nThis Lemonade case study examines how a young insurtech rebuilt insurance around AI instead of digitizing an old process. Lemonade sells policies through a chatbot in minutes, pays some claims in seconds through another, and runs underwriting on machine learning, turning one of the slowest, least-trusted industries into a fast, modern digital insurance experience. Just as important, its flat-fee model removes the usual incentive to deny claims, which builds trust and reduces fraud. The result is a transformed insurance customer experience and a business growing quickly toward profitability. The Lemonade insurance story shows what it means to redesign an industry around AI and aligned incentives, not just a better app.\n\n### Lemonade rebuilt insurance around AI, selling policies through a chatbot in minutes and paying some claims in seconds, on a flat-fee model that removes the usual incentive to deny you.\n\nInsurance is one of the industries people trust least and enjoy least. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPYMNTS | Liberate’s AI Agents Take Over the Insurance Night Shift (https://www.pymnts.com/insurance/2026/liberates-ai-agents-take-over-the-insurance-night-shift/)\nciteturn1search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 days ago; Crawled: today; Liberate’s agents let customers buy insurance when human agents typically aren’t available, Singh told TechCrunch in October 2025. ... One property and casualty insurer handled 14 times its usual claim volume during back-to-back hurricanes without adding staff, using Liberate’s digital first-notice-of-loss intake, the company reported in a case study. ... As of March, regulators in 12 states were piloting an AI evaluation tool, with full adoption expected at the NAIC’s 2026 Fall National Meeting.\n\n# Liberate’s AI Agents Take Over the Insurance Night Shift\n\nBy PYMNTS October 2, 2026\n\nImage: AI, insurance\n\nHighlights\n\nLiberate’s AI agents have given insurers back more than 100 million minutes. \n\nThe agents let customers buy coverage 24/7, and one insurer handled 14 times its usual claim volume during back-to-back hurricanes without adding staff.\n\nBranch cut claim reporting time 42% with Liberate’s AI agents.\n\nA policyholder calls her insurance agency to ask what she owes. An artificial intelligence (AI) agent answers, pulls the balance and logs the call in the carrier’s core system, with no employee on the line. Interactions like that have given insurance agencies and carriers back more than 100 million minutes, Liberate, a provider of AI agents for property and casualty insurers, said Wednesday (Sept. 30).\n\nLiberate’s agents handle requests that don’t need human judgment, such as a contractor asking for a certificate of insurance or a policyholder checking on an open claim, the company said. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAI Underwriting: Zurich Submission Review Case Study | OneShot (https://oneshotagent.com/enterprise/case-studies/zurich-underwriting-submissions)\nciteturn1search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Insurance · Case study ... By OneShot · Updated October 4, 2026 ... Zurich adopted AI-assisted drafting tailored to its risk appetite and preferred format.\n\nImageZurich North America\n\nInsurance · Case study\n\n# Zurich: prepare underwriting files\n\nMove commercial submissions from scattered documents to a cited narrative an underwriter can review.\n\nSolution design based on public sources. Reported results are attributed to their original deployments.\n\nBy OneShot · Updated October 4, 2026\n\nWhat the workflow produces\n\nUnderwriting review file\n\nSubmission checklist\n    Required documents, versions and missing items.\n\nRisk narrative\n    Exposures and loss history with document citations.\n\nOpen questions\n    Broker requests and material contradictions.\n\nReviewer record\n    Underwriter edits, conditions and disposition.\n\n## The documented problem\n\nThe published Zurich North America case study describes middle-market underwriters reviewing extensive exposures, loss runs and supplemental forms before writing underwriting narratives. The repeated document work consumed time needed for risk judgment and broker relationships.\n\nZurich adopted AI-assisted drafting tailored to its risk appetite and preferred format. The OneShot workflow below combines document analysis, broker follow-up and review records in the agent’s tool basket to carry a submission through to the underwriter’s decision.\n\n  * Zurich North America underwriting case studyPublished case study · Checked 2026-10-04\n\n### Reported result from the existing deployment\n\nThe cited case study reports that Zurich underwriters saved an average of two hours per submission. This is attributed to the documented deployment, not to OneShot.\n\nThe OneShot solution\n\n## Underwriting submissions ready for human review\n\nKeep each commercial submission complete, traceable and ready for an underwriter’s decision.\n\nWork enters the queue\n    A new broker submission or a material document revision.\n\nDecision owner\n    Assigned underwriter\n\n  1. 01\n\n### Check the submission\n\nMatch incoming documents to the insurer’s required checklist. Identify missing loss runs, supplemental forms and inconsistent policy periods; request the precise missing items from the broker.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInvestor Relations | FF EAI Robotics Ecosystem Inc. (https://investors.ff.com/)\nciteturn1search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; October 5, 2026 ... October 1, 2026 ... Report of unscheduled material events or corporate event ... (NASDAQ: FFR, renamed from AIxCrypto Holdings, Inc. effective September 30, 2026) is a U.S.-based Embodied AI (EAI) robotics company that is in the process of acquiring the FF EAI Robotics business. ... The ecosystem is designed to support the full robotics lifecycle, including R&D, deployment, data collection and training, operations, and commercial applications.\n\nSeptember 30, 2026\n\nAIxCrypto Holdings, Inc. Announces Name Change to FF EAI Robotics Ecosystem Inc. and Ticker Symbol Change to FFR\n\n  * All News Releases\n\n## Events\n\n### Past Events\n\nAugust 7, 2026 | 7:30 PM EDT\n\nAIxCryp